   196	## 3. Elementary lemmas (all proved here)
   197	
   198	**E1 (orthogonality majorant).** For (a, q) = 1, E(z, y; q, a) = φ(q)^{−1} Σ_{χ≠χ₀ (mod q)} χ̄(a)Ψ(z, y; χ).
   199	Hence max_{(a,q)=1} |E(z, y; q, a)| ≤ Δ(q) := φ(q)^{−1} Σ_{χ≠χ₀} |Ψ(z, y; χ)|.
   200	*Proof.* Orthogonality, together with the fact that Ψ(z, y; χ₀) = Ψ_q(z, y). ∎
   201	
   202	**E2 (lift averaging).** Let ℓ be a prime with ℓ > y, let s ≥ 1 with ℓ ∤ s, and put t = ℓs. For every
   203	r with (r, t) = 1:
   204	  E(z, y; t, r) = E(z, y; s, r)/(ℓ − 1) + Rem(t, r),
   205	  where |Rem(t, r)| ≤ Δ_ℓ(t) := φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ (mod t), ℓ | cond χ} |Ψ(z, y; χ)|.
   206	
   207	*Proof.* (ℤ/t)^* = (ℤ/ℓ)^* × (ℤ/s)^*, and every χ (mod t) factors as χ_ℓχ_s. Also ℓ | cond χ if and
   208	only if χ_ℓ ≠ χ₀^{(ℓ)}.
   209	- Every y-smooth n is coprime to ℓ. So for χ_ℓ = χ₀^{(ℓ)} we get Ψ(z, y; χ₀^{(ℓ)}χ_s) = Ψ(z, y; χ_s).
   210	  Also Ψ_t(z, y) = Ψ_s(z, y), and φ(t) = (ℓ − 1)φ(s).
   211	- In E1 for modulus t, the terms with χ_ℓ = χ₀^{(ℓ)} and χ_s ≠ χ₀^{(s)} sum to
   212	  ((ℓ−1)φ(s))^{−1} Σ_{χ_s≠χ₀} χ̄_s(r)Ψ(z, y; χ_s) = E(z, y; s, r)/(ℓ − 1).
   213	- The remaining terms have ℓ | cond χ.
   214	
   215	Equivalently, without characters: averaging E(z, y; t, ·) over the ℓ − 1 lifts of r (mod s) to
   216	(ℤ/t)^* gives exactly E(z, y; s, r)/(ℓ − 1). This is checked exactly in `tests/test_lem85.py`. ∎
   217	
   218	**E3 (coprimality by Möbius).** Let χ (mod q) be induced by a primitive χ* (mod r), and put
   219	q_y := ∏_{p|q, p≤y} p. Then
   220	  Ψ(z, y; χ) = Σ_{d | q_y, (d,r)=1} μ(d)χ*(d)Ψ(z/d, y; χ*).
   221	
   222	*Proof.* For y-smooth n, (n, q) = 1 if and only if (n, q_y) = 1. Expand 1_{(n,q_y)=1} = Σ_{d|(n,q_y)} μ(d).
   223	Then n = dn′ with n′ ∈ S(z/d, y), because d is y-smooth. Terms with (d, r) > 1 vanish. Checked
   224	exactly for real characters in the tests. ∎
   225	
   226	**E4 (factorisation).** Let M₁ ≥ 1. Every n ∈ S(z, y) with n > M₁ has exactly one factorisation n = mk
   227	with:
   228	- M₁ < m ≤ P⁺(m)M₁ (that is, m/P⁺(m) ≤ M₁ < m);
   229	- P⁺(m) ≤ P⁻(k);
   230	- k ∈ S(z, y).
   231	
   232	Moreover k ≤ z/M₁.
   233	
   234	*Proof.* Write n = p₁p₂⋯p_J with p₁ ≤ ⋯ ≤ p_J, and let m = p₁⋯p_j with j minimal such that the product
   235	exceeds M₁. Conversely, if (m, k) satisfies the three conditions, the primes of m come first in the
   236	sorted list of n's primes (ties at P⁺(m) = P⁻(k) are harmless). Then m/P⁺(m) ≤ M₁ < m forces j to be
   237	the number of prime factors of m. Checked exhaustively on toy ranges in the tests. ∎
   238	
   239	**E5.** Σ_{j≤X} 1/φ(j) ≤ 2(1 + log X) for X ≥ 1.
   240	*Proof.* j/φ(j) = Σ_{e|j} μ²(e)/φ(e). So the sum is at most (1 + log X)·Σ_e μ²(e)/(eφ(e)), and
   241	Σ_e μ²(e)/(eφ(e)) = ζ(2)ζ(3)/ζ(6) < 1.95. ∎
   242	
   243	**E6 (constants).** Over squarefree d:
   244	- Σ_d 1/(dφ(d)) < 1.95;
   245	- Σ_d 1/(φ(d)d^{1/2}) ≤ ∏_p (1 + 2p^{−3/2}) ≤ exp(2Σ_p p^{−3/2}) < e^{1.7} < 6, since Σ_p p^{−3/2} < 0.85;
   246	- if β, β′ ≥ 3/4, then Σ_d d^{1−β−β′}/φ(d) ≤ ∏_p (1 + p^{−1/2}/(p − 1)) < 6.
   247	
   248	Also n/φ(n) ≤ 5 log log(n + 16) for all n ≥ 1, by (RS) Theorem 15 for n ≥ 16 and by inspection for
   249	n < 16.
   250	
   251	**E7 (density lower bound).** For z ≥ 2y with u_z := log z/log y ≤ u and N ≥ N₀:
   252	  ϱ(z, y) ≥ y^{−1}exp(−u log(4u log y)).
   253	In regime (R), u log(4u log y) = (ξ/a²)(1 + o(1)) log y. Hence ϱ(z, y) ≥ y^{−1−ξ_max/a²−o(1)}.
   254	
   255	*Proof.* Let k := ⌊u_z⌋ and 𝒫 := primes in (y/2, y]. By (RS) Cor. 3, #𝒫 ≥ 3y/(10 log y) ≥ k. The
   256	products of k distinct primes of 𝒫 are distinct, y-smooth, and ≤ y^k ≤ z. So
   257	  Ψ(z, y) ≥ C(#𝒫, k) ≥ (#𝒫/k)^k ≥ (z/y)(3/(10k log y))^k.
   258	Then use k ≤ u. In regime (R), log y = a(log N log log N)^{1/2}(1 + o(1)) and
   259	u = (ξ/a)(log N/log log N)^{1/2}(1 + o(1)). ∎
   260	
   261	**E8 (weights over smooth moduli).** Let 2y ≤ Q′ ≤ x, r, d ≥ 1 with (r, d) = 1, and β′ := β(Q′, y).
   262	Then
   263	  W(r, d) := Σ_{Q′/2<s≤Q′, P⁺(s)≤y, rd|s} 1/φ(s) ≤ 10 C_dBT log log(Q′ + 16)·ϱ(Q′, y)·(rd)^{1−β′}/(φ(r)φ(d)).
   264	
   265	*Proof.*
   266	- Write s = rdj, so that 1/φ(s) ≤ 1/(φ(rd)φ(j)) and φ(rd) = φ(r)φ(d). Here j is y-smooth and
   267	  j ∈ (J/2, J] with J := Q′/(rd); the sum is empty if J < 1.
   268	- By E6, Σ_j 1/φ(j) ≤ 5 log log(Q′ + 16)·(2/J)Ψ(J, y) = 10 log log(Q′ + 16)·ϱ(J, y).
   269	- By (dBT) at (Q′, y) with d := rd: ϱ(J, y) = Ψ(Q′/(rd), y)·rd/Q′ ≤ C_dBT ϱ(Q′, y)(rd)^{1−β′}. ∎
   270	
   271	---
   272	
   273	## 4. Lemma LS (large conductors, from the classical large sieve)
   274	
   275	**Lemma LS.** Let y ≥ 2, z ≥ max(4, y^{3/2}) and R ≥ 1. Then
   276	  Σ_{R<r≤2R} (r/φ(r)) Σ*_{χ (mod r)} |Ψ(z, y; χ)| ≤ 30π(y) log(2zR)·(y^{3/2}z + y^{1/2}z^{1/2}R²).
   277	
   278	*Proof.* Write w_χ := r/φ(r) for χ primitive of conductor r ∈ (R, 2R]. Then Σ_χ w_χ ≤ Σ_{r≤2R} r ≤ 4R².
   279	Put M₁ := z^{1/2}. By E4,
   280	  Ψ(z, y; χ) = Σ_{n≤M₁, n∈S(y)} χ(n) + Σ_{p≤y} Σ_{m,k: mk≤z} a^{(p)}_m b^{(p)}_k χ(m)χ(k),
   281	where:
   282	- a^{(p)}_m := 1 if P⁺(m) = p and M₁ < m ≤ pM₁, and 0 otherwise;
   283	- b^{(p)}_k := 1 if P⁻(k) ≥ p, P⁺(k) ≤ y and k ≤ z/M₁, and 0 otherwise.
   284	
   285	Neither sequence depends on χ, and #supp a^{(p)} ≤ yM₁, #supp b^{(p)} ≤ z/M₁.
   286	
   287	*Short part.* By Cauchy–Schwarz and (LS) with Q = 2R:
   288	  Σ_χ w_χ |Σ_{n≤M₁}| ≤ (4R²)^{1/2}((4R² + M₁)M₁)^{1/2} ≤ 2Rz^{1/2} + 4R²z^{1/4}.
   289	
   290	*Bilinear part.*
   291	- Let z′ := ⌊z⌋ + 1/2, c := 1/log(2z) and T := (2zR)^4. For integers m, k ≥ 1, 1_{mk≤z} = 1_{z′/(mk)>1}.
   292	- On the support, mk ≤ (yM₁)(z/M₁) = yz and |z′ − mk| ≥ 1/2. Hence |log(z′/mk)| ≥ |z′−mk|/max(z′, mk)
   293	  ≥ 1/(4yz), and (z′/mk)^c ≤ (2z)^c = e.
   294	- By (P), each pair (m, k) contributes an error ≤ 4eyz/(πT) < 4eyz/T. There are ≤ yz pairs per p, at most y
   295	  primes p, and total character weight ≤ 4R². So the total error over all χ and p is
   296	  < 16e y³z²R²/T ≤ e·y³/(z²R²) ≤ 3.
   297	- The main term is (2πi)^{−1}∫_{c−iT}^{c+iT} A_p(χ, s)B_p(χ, s) z′^s ds/s, where
   298	  A_p(χ, s) = Σ_m a^{(p)}_m χ(m)m^{−s} and B_p is defined similarly.
   299	- For each fixed t, Cauchy–Schwarz and (LS) with coefficients a_m m^{−c−it} (of modulus ≤ 1) give
   300	    Σ_χ w_χ |A_p(χ, c+it)B_p(χ, c+it)| ≤ (yM₁ + 4R²)^{1/2}(z/M₁ + 4R²)^{1/2}(yM₁)^{1/2}(z/M₁)^{1/2}
   301	      ≤ (yz^{1/2} + 4R²)(yz)^{1/2} = y^{3/2}z + 4y^{1/2}z^{1/2}R².
   302	- Also ∫_{−T}^{T} dt/|c + it| ≤ 2/c + 2 log T ≤ 10 log(2zR) and z′^c ≤ e. So the bilinear part is
   303	  ≤ 5π(y) log(2zR)(y^{3/2}z + 4y^{1/2}z^{1/2}R²) + 3.
   304	
   305	Adding the two parts, and using 2Rz^{1/2} + 4R²z^{1/4} + 3 ≤ 9y^{1/2}z^{1/2}R² + y^{3/2}z, gives the
   306	bound. ∎
   307	
   308	**Corollary LS′ (moduli with smooth cofactor, any sub-family).** Let z₀ ≥ max(4, y^{3/2}), and let
   309	1 ≤ R₀ ≤ Q₀ with z₀/Q₀ ≥ max(4, y^{3/2}). For each modulus q ≤ Q₀ let 𝒳(q) be any set of characters
   310	mod q whose conductors lie in (R₀, Q₀]. Then
   311	  Σ_{q≤Q₀} φ(q)^{−1} Σ_{χ∈𝒳(q)} |Ψ(z₀, y; χ)| ≤ 1.5·10³ π(y)(log z₀)²·(y^{3/2}z₀/R₀ + y^{1/2}z₀^{1/2}Q₀).
   312	
   313	*Proof.*
   314	- Group (q, χ) by the inducing primitive χ* (mod r) and apply E3: |Ψ(z₀, y; χ)| ≤ Σ_{d|q_y, (d,r)=1}
   315	  |Ψ(z₀/d, y; χ*)|, with d squarefree and d ≤ Q₀.
   316	- Swap the sums. By E5 and φ(rdj) ≥ φ(r)φ(d)φ(j), Σ_{q≤Q₀, rd|q} 1/φ(q) ≤ 2(1 + log Q₀)/(φ(r)φ(d)).
   317	- On each dyadic block r ∈ (R, 2R] with R₀ ≤ R ≤ Q₀, use 1/φ(r) ≤ (r/φ(r))/R and Lemma LS at
   318	  z = z₀/d ≥ z₀/Q₀. This gives 30π(y)·2 log z₀·(y^{3/2}z/R + y^{1/2}z^{1/2}R).
   319	  (Here log(2zR) ≤ log(2z₀Q₀) ≤ 2 log z₀, because Q₀ ≤ z₀/4.)
   320	- Sum over the blocks R = 2^iR₀ (Σ1/R ≤ 2/R₀, ΣR ≤ 2Q₀), then over d with weights 1/φ(d), using
   321	  Σ_d 1/(dφ(d)) < 2 and Σ_d 1/(φ(d)d^{1/2}) < 6 (E6). This gives
   322	    ≤ 2(1 + log Q₀)·60π(y) log z₀·(4y^{3/2}z₀/R₀ + 12y^{1/2}z₀^{1/2}Q₀),
   323	  and 1 + log Q₀ ≤ log z₀ because eQ₀ ≤ z₀. ∎
   324	
   325	---
   326	
   327	## 5. Lemma SM (smooth moduli, relative saving)
   328	
   329	**Lemma SM.** In regime (R), let 2y ≤ Q′ ≤ x^{1/2−λ}, and let 𝒮 := {s ∈ (Q′/2, Q′] : P⁺(s) ≤ y}. Then
   330	for N ≥ N₀:
   331	  Σ_{s∈𝒮} max_{(a,s)=1} |E(x, y; s, a)| ≤ Σ_{s∈𝒮} Δ(s) ≤ C_SM·Ψ(x, y)·ϱ(Q′, y)·ε_N.
   332	
   333	*Proof.* The first inequality is E1. Split each Δ(s) according to the conductor r of χ: case (a) is
   334	1 < r ≤ R₁ = y^K, and case (b) is r > R₁.
   335	
   336	**(a) Small conductors.**
   337	- Apply E3 (s is y-smooth, so s_y = rad s) and swap the sums. W(r, d) = 0 unless rd ≤ Q′, so only
   338	  d ≤ Q′ occurs. With W(r, d) from E8:
   339	    (a) ≤ Σ_{1<r≤R₁} Σ*_{χ* (mod r)} Σ_{d≤Q′ sqfree, P⁺(d)≤y, (d,r)=1} |Ψ(x/d, y; χ*)|·W(r, d)
   340	        ≤ 10 C_dBT log log(Q′+16)·ϱ(Q′, y)·R₁^{1−β′}·Σ_{d≤Q′} (d^{1−β′}/φ(d))·D(x/d),
   341	    where D(z) := Σ_{1<r≤R₁} φ(r)^{−1} Σ*_{χ (mod r)} |Ψ(z, y; χ)|.
   342	  The cutoff d ≤ Q′ is kept through the next two steps, (D) and (dBT). Only the final Euler sum is
   343	  extended to all squarefree d, by positivity. (Fix from codex lem85_1 #2 and the Opus proof critic #5.)
   344	- *Apply (D) to the primitive characters only.* Take q = r ≤ R₁ and χ = χ* primitive. These pairs
   345	  are among the terms of Lemme 5's left side with Q := R₁. Take A = 1/2 and the argument z = x/d.
   346	  - The hypotheses hold: x/d ≥ x/Q′ ≥ x^{1/2}, so (log z)^{c_D} ≤ y ≤ z and R₁ = y^K =
   347	    N^{o(1)} ≤ z^{η_D} for N ≥ N₀.
   348	  - Hence D(x/d) ≤ C_D Ψ(x/d, y)(H(u_d)^{−δ_D} + y^{−δ_D}), where u_d := log(x/d)/log y ≥ u/2
   349	    (because d ≤ Q′ ≤ x^{1/2}).
   350	  - H is increasing on [4, ∞), so H(u_d) ≥ H(u/2).
   351	- By (dBT) (d ≤ Q′ ≤ x), Ψ(x/d, y) ≤ C_dBT Ψ(x, y)d^{−β}, with β := β(x, y). By (α1) and E6,
   352	  extending to all squarefree d, Σ_{d≤Q′} d^{1−β′−β}/φ(d) < 6.
   353	- By (α2), R₁^{1−β′} = exp(K(1 − β′) log y) ≤ (u log y)^K (log y)^{KC_α} ≤ (log x)^{K(1+C_α)}.
   354	- Therefore
   355	    (a) ≤ 60 C_dBT² C_D·log log x·(log x)^{K(1+C_α)}·(e^{−δ_D (u/2)/log²(u/2+1)} + y^{−δ_D})·Ψ(x, y)ϱ(Q′, y).
   356	- In regime (R), u = (ξ/a)(log N/log log N)^{1/2}(1 + o(1)) and log²(u + 1) = (1/4)(log log N)²(1 + o(1)).
   357	  So δ_D(u/2)/log²(u/2+1) = (2δ_Dξ/a)(log N)^{1/2}/(log log N)^{5/2}·(1 + o(1)).
   358	  - This beats the (log x)^{O(1)} = exp(O(log log N)) factor and the threshold 2c₀ = δ_D min Ξ/a.
   359	  - y^{−δ_D} = exp(−δ_D a(log N log log N)^{1/2}) is smaller still.
   360	- Hence (a) ≤ Ψ(x, y)ϱ(Q′, y)ε_N for N ≥ N₀.
   361	
   362	**(b) Large conductors.**
   363	- If Q′ ≤ R₁, case (b) is empty: every conductor divides s ≤ Q′. (Fix from codex #3 and Opus #6.)
   364	  Otherwise apply Corollary LS′ with z₀ = x, Q₀ = Q′, R₀ = R₁ and 𝒳(s) = {χ (mod s) : cond χ > R₁}, keeping
   365	  only the moduli s ∈ 𝒮 (positivity). This gives
   366	    (b) ≤ 1.5·10³π(y)(log x)²(y^{3/2}x/y^K + y^{1/2}x^{1/2}Q′).
   367	- By E7, Ψ(x, y)ϱ(Q′, y) ≥ x·y^{−2−2ξ_max/a²−o(1)}.
   368	  - The first term is ≤ Ψϱ(Q′)·y^{5/2−K+2+2ξ_max/a²+o(1)} ≤ Ψϱ(Q′)y^{−1}, because
   369	    K = 6 + ⌈2ξ_max/a²⌉.
   370	  - The second is ≤ Ψϱ(Q′)·x^{−λ}y^{O(1)}, because Q′ ≤ x^{1/2−λ}.
   371	  - Both are ≤ Ψϱ(Q′)ε_N for N ≥ N₀. ∎
   372	
   373	*Why this works exactly at y = L[1/2].*
   374	- The large sieve needs R₁ ≥ 1/(ϱ(x)ϱ(Q′)) up to y^{O(1)}, that is, log R₁ ≳ u log u.
   375	- The small-conductor transfer costs R₁^{1−β} = exp(log R₁·log(u log y)/log y).
   376	- Both are compatible with log R₁ ≍ log y precisely when u log u ≍ log y, i.e. y = L^{Θ(1)}.
   377	- For much smaller y one would need the medium-conductor zero-density analysis of Harper/LV; we
   378	  avoid it.
   379	
   380	---
   381	
   382	## 6. Proof of Lemma 8.5′
   383	
   384	*(i) Counting 𝒯_Q.*
   385	- Since ℓ > y ≥ P⁺(s), each t ∈ 𝒯_Q determines (ℓ, s), and ℓ ∤ s. So
   386	  #𝒯_Q = Σ_{ℓ∈J} (Ψ(Q/ℓ, y) − Ψ(Q/2ℓ, y)).
   387	- Upper bound: #𝒯_Q ≤ #J·Ψ(Q/Y, y) ≤ (1.256·2Y/log 2Y)(Q/Y)ϱ(Q/Y, y) ≤ 3Qδ_𝒯, by (RS) Cor. 1.
   388	- Lower bound:
   389	  - Put z := Q/(2ℓ) ≥ x^λ/4. Then u_z → ∞ and α(z, y) → 1, by (HT) Theorem 2(i).
   390	  - (HT) Theorem 3 with c = 2 gives Ψ(2z, y) − Ψ(z, y) ≥ 0.8Ψ(z, y). With c = 4 it gives
   391	    Ψ(Q/Y, y) ≤ 4.1Ψ(Q/4Y, y) ≤ 4.1Ψ(z, y).
   392	  - So each ℓ contributes ≥ 0.19(Q/Y)ϱ(Q/Y, y).
   393	  - #J ≥ 3Y/(5 log Y), by (RS) Cor. 3. Hence #𝒯_Q ≥ 0.11Qδ_𝒯.
   394	
   395	*(ii) The error sum.* By E2, for t = ℓs ∈ 𝒯_Q,
   396	  max_{(r,t)=1} |E(x, y; t, r)| ≤ max_{(a,s)=1} |E(x, y; s, a)|/(ℓ − 1) + Δ_ℓ(t).
   397	
   398	**Part 1 (χ_ℓ principal).**
   399	- Σ_t max_a |E(x, y; s, a)|/(ℓ − 1) = Σ_{ℓ∈J} (ℓ − 1)^{−1} Σ_{s∈(Q/2ℓ, Q/ℓ], P⁺(s)≤y} max_a |E(x, y; s, a)|.
   400	- Apply Lemma SM with Q′ = Q/ℓ. Its hypotheses hold: Q/ℓ ≥ Q/(2Y) ≥ x^λ/2 ≥ 2y and
   401	  Q/ℓ < Q ≤ x^{1/2−λ}.
   402	- This gives ≤ C_SM Ψ(x, y)ε_N Σ_ℓ ϱ(Q/ℓ, y)/(ℓ − 1).
   403	- Ψ(·, y) is nondecreasing and ℓ ∈ (Y, 2Y], so ϱ(Q/ℓ, y) = Ψ(Q/ℓ, y)ℓ/Q ≤ Ψ(Q/Y, y)·2Y/Q =
   404	  2ϱ(Q/Y, y). Also Σ_{ℓ∈J} 1/(ℓ − 1) ≤ #J/Y ≤ 2.6/log Y, by (RS) Cor. 1.
   405	- So **Part 1 ≤ 5.2 C_SM·Ψ(x, y)δ_𝒯ε_N.**
   406	
   407	**Part 2 (ℓ | conductor).**
   408	- Every χ counted in Δ_ℓ(t) has conductor r with ℓ | r, so r > Y.
   409	- Apply Corollary LS′ with z₀ = x, Q₀ = Q, R₀ = Y and 𝒳(t) = {χ : ℓ_t | cond χ} for t ∈ 𝒯_Q (empty
   410	  for other q). This gives Part 2 ≤ 1.5·10³π(y)(log x)²(y^{3/2}x/Y + y^{1/2}x^{1/2}Q).
   411	- By E7, Ψ(x, y)δ_𝒯 ≥ x·y^{−2−2ξ_max/a²−o(1)}/log Y. With Y ≥ y^K and Q ≤ x^{1/2−λ} this gives
   412	  **Part 2 ≤ Ψ(x, y)δ_𝒯·y^{−1/2} ≤ Ψ(x, y)δ_𝒯ε_N** for N ≥ N₀.
   413	
   414	Adding Parts 1 and 2 proves (ii) with C := 5.2 C_SM + 1. ∎
   415	
