   627	## 13. Why not below 2/5 in this design
   628	
   629	These are statements about this algorithm family (one-level special-q descent of Dixon-split primes), not lower
   630	bounds for factoring.
   631	- (R1) [heuristic upper bound on the split] The shape test accepts only if |u₀|, |v₀| ≤ √(2N) are products of ≤ k
   632	  primes of size N^{1/(2k)}; the density of such integers is about (C/log N)^k each, so the expected number of
   633	  attempts per row is ≥ (log N/C)^{2k}, and L[α] cost forces k = O(D^α).
   634	- (R3) [proved for the design] θ = 2μ + h + (d−1)ν with h ≥ 2ν and the window/level floors gives θ ≥ max(3/(d+1),
   635	  (d+1)ν) − o(1), so θ² ≥ 3/(4k)(1 − o(1)); and the smoothness cost of norms N^{θ} at B = L[α] is
   636	  exp((1 − α)θ log N·log log N/log B·(1+o(1))) by CEP, which is L[α]^{O(1)} only if θ = O(D^{2α−1}).
   637	- Together k ≳ D^{2(1−2α)} and k = O(D^α), so α ≥ 2/5. Theorem U attains it.
   638	- Below 2/5 one needs multi-level descent and a lower bound for smooth values over a field-dependent good set
   639	  (endogenous children; recurse §3.4). Agent `nested` (phase 12) works on that under ERH. Nothing here helps.
