    68	
    69	## 1. Statement
    70	
    71	Notation.
    72	- P⁺(n) and P⁻(n) are the largest and smallest prime factors of n, with P⁺(1) = 1 and P⁻(1) = +∞.
    73	- S(z, y) is the set of y-smooth n ≤ z, Ψ(z, y) = #S(z, y), and ϱ(z, y) := Ψ(z, y)/z.
    74	- For a Dirichlet character χ of any modulus, Ψ(z, y; χ) := Σ_{n∈S(z,y)} χ(n). Here χ(n) = 0 when
    75	  (n, mod χ) > 1.
    76	- For (a, q) = 1: Ψ(z, y; q, a) := #{n ∈ S(z, y) : n ≡ a (mod q)}, Ψ_q(z, y) := #{n ∈ S(z, y) :
    77	  (n, q) = 1}, and E(z, y; q, a) := Ψ(z, y; q, a) − Ψ_q(z, y)/φ(q).
    78	- u := log x/log y, and H(u) := exp(u/log²(u + 1)) (Drappeau's normalisation).
    79	- α(z, y) is the Hildebrand–Tenenbaum saddle point: the solution of Σ_{p≤y} log p/(p^α − 1) = log z.
    80	- L = L_N[1/2, 1] = exp((log N log log N)^{1/2}).
    81	
    82	**Regime (R).** Fix a > 0, a compact Ξ ⊂ (0, ∞) with ξ_max := max Ξ, and λ ∈ (0, 1/4).
    83	- N → ∞, y := ⌊L_N[1/2, a]⌋ (this is B), and x is real with log x/log N ∈ Ξ.
    84	- K := 6 + ⌈2ξ_max/a²⌉ and R₁ := y^K.
    85	- ε_N := exp(−c₀ (log N)^{1/2}/(log log N)^{5/2}). Here c₀ := δ_D·min Ξ/(2a) > 0, and δ_D is the
    86	  constant of Drappeau's Lemme 5 (§2, D).
    87	
    88	**Lemma 8.5′.** In regime (R), let Y, Q be reals with y^K ≤ Y and x^λ·Y ≤ Q ≤ x^{1/2−λ}. Put
    89	- J := {ℓ prime : Y < ℓ ≤ 2Y};
    90	- 𝒯_Q := {t = ℓs ∈ (Q/2, Q] : ℓ ∈ J, P⁺(s) ≤ y};
    91	- δ_𝒯 := ϱ(Q/Y, y)/log Y.
    92	
    93	Then there are N₀, C > 0, depending only on (a, Ξ, λ) and on the constants of the results in §2, all
    94	effective, such that for N ≥ N₀:
    95	- (i) Qδ_𝒯/10 ≤ #𝒯_Q ≤ 3Qδ_𝒯;
    96	- (ii) Σ_{t∈𝒯_Q} max_{(r,t)=1} |Ψ(x, y; t, r) − Ψ_t(x, y)/φ(t)| ≤ C·Ψ(x, y)·δ_𝒯·ε_N.
    97	
    98	*Remarks on the statement.*
    99	- This contains pcest §2.2's Lemma 8.5′ (regime (B), which is what Theorem A uses), with ε_N in
   100	  place of H(u)^{−c} + B^{−c} + x^{−η₂}.
   101	  - In regime (R), H(u)^{−c} = exp(−c′(log N)^{1/2}/(log log N)^{5/2}(1 + o(1))), so the two forms of
   102	    ε_N are of the same type.
   103	  - FM Step 3 (pcest §2.3) needs only (log ω)·ε_N·log log N → 0 with log ω = O((log N log log N)^{1/2}).
   104	    This holds.
   105	- The condition y^K ≤ Y also covers hypD's regime (A), Y = exp((log N)^{3/4}). Regime (A) is not used
   106	  by Theorem A.
   107	- Interface with the final design (thmA_proof §1):
   108	  - Y = 2^{j−1} = N^{2ν+o(1)}, Q = N^{μ+ν+o(1)}, Q/Y = N^{μ−ν+o(1)} with μ − ν ≥ η₀, and
   109	    x = N^{ξ+o(1)} with ξ = μ + h + dν. Also ξ/2 − (μ + ν) ≥ η₀/2 (the FM level-½ floor).
   110	  - So the hypotheses hold with λ := η₀/(4ξ_max), Ξ a small closed neighbourhood of ξ, and a = θ/√2.
   111	    They hold uniformly over FM's grid 𝒳 and its O(1) dyadic Q, because ω = L^{C₁} changes log x
   112	    by o(log N).
   113	
   114	---
   115	
   116	## 2. Cited results (exact statements; only these are used)
   117	
   118	All are published. "Checked" means we read the statement in the source, or in an author copy whose
   119	numbering matches the published version.
   120	
   121	- **(D) Drappeau, *Théorèmes de type Fouvry–Iwaniec pour les entiers friables*, Compos. Math. 151
   122	  (2015), no. 5, 828–862, Lemme 5.**
   123	  - Statement: there exist constants η_D, c_D, δ_D > 0 such that whenever (log x)^{c_D} ≤ y ≤ x and
   124	    Q ≤ x, for every fixed A > 0,
   125	      Σ_{q≤Q} φ(q)^{−1} Σ_{χ (mod q), 1<cond(χ)≤x^{η_D}} |Σ_{n∈S(x,y)} χ(n)|
   126	        ≪_A Ψ(x, y){H(u)^{−δ_D}(log x)^{−A} + y^{−δ_D}}.
   127	  - "La constante implicite est effective si A < 1."
   128	  - Checked in arXiv 1307.7554v3 and in the author's copy (bffi-friable.pdf, numbering identical).
   129	  - The same statement appears as **Pascadi, *Smooth numbers in arithmetic progressions to large
   130	    moduli*, Compos. Math. 161 (2025), no. 8, 1923–1974, Lemma 3.2**. Pascadi gives the range
   131	    (log x)^C ≤ y ≤ x, Q ≤ x, conductor ≤ x^ε, and "effective if A < 1". Checked in arXiv
   132	    2304.11696v3. Pascadi cites it as "[7, Lemme 5]", where [7] is the Compositio version.
   133	  - **Provenance.** Drappeau writes only "il découle des calculs de la section 3.3 de [Har12b]"; Pascadi
   134	    writes "follows from the work of Harper in [21, Section 3.3]".
   135	    - *Our reading*, not either author's statement: the relevant computation is Harper's eq. (3.1),
   136	      the effective bound Σ_{1<r≤x^η} Σ*_{χ*} Σ_{q≤Q} φ(q)^{−1} Σ_{χ induced by χ*} |Ψ(x, y; χ)| ≪
   137	      Ψ(x, y)(e^{−cu/log²(u+1)} + y^{−c}), with an ineffective (log x)^{−A} strengthening asserted after it.
   138	    - Harper's literal context is that of his Theorems 1–2 (Q ≤ √Ψ(x, y), log^K x ≤ y). Drappeau's
   139	      "Q ≤ x" is a legitimate reading of the computation, since it uses only Σ_{s≤Q/r} 1/φ(s) ≪ log x.
   140	      The "effective if A < 1" form is Drappeau's addition.
   141	    - **Our use lies inside even Harper's literal context.** We take Q = R₁ = y^K ≤ √Ψ(x/d, y), and we
   142	      discard the (log x)^{−A} factor entirely, so Harper's effective form (3.1) is all that is needed.
   143	      We take **A = 1/2** only to quote Drappeau's effective statement.
   144	- **(dBT) Drappeau 2015 (as in (D)), Lemme 1(i).** (α is the saddle point. The citation critic
   145	  confirmed this from the proof of dBT's (2·19), via their Lemme 5.1.)
   146	  - Statement: "Lorsque 2 ⩽ y ⩽ x et 1 ⩽ d ⩽ x, on a Ψ(x/d, y) ≪ Ψ(x, y)/d^α", where α = α(x, y) is
   147	    the saddle point of Drappeau's (1.6) (= HT86 (3.1)). Checked in arXiv v3 and the author's copy.
   148	  - Drappeau derives it from de la Bretèche–Tenenbaum, Ramanujan J. 9 (2005), 139–202,
   149	    Théorème 2.4(i) with m = 1. For m = 1, ω(m) ≪ 1 holds, which allows the full range 1 ≤ d ≤ x.
   150	    - The dBT author copy we read says it includes corrections relative to the published version
   151	      ("Nous incluons ici certaines corrections par rapport à la version publiée"). So we anchor the
   152	      citation to Drappeau's published, numbered Lemme 1(i), and give dBT only as its source.
   153	  - We write C_dBT for the implied constant (absolute).
   154	- **(HT) Hildebrand–Tenenbaum, *On integers free of large prime factors*, Trans. AMS 296 (1986),
   155	  265–290.** Checked (scan).
   156	  - Theorem 2(i), eq. (2.4): α(x, y) = (log(1 + y/log x)/log y)·(1 + O(log log(1 + y)/log y)),
   157	    uniformly for x ≥ y ≥ 2.
   158	  - Theorem 3: Ψ(cx, y) = Ψ(x, y)c^{α(x,y)}(1 + O(1/u + log y/y)), uniformly for x ≥ y ≥ 2 and
   159	    1 ≤ c ≤ y.
   160	- **(LS) Iwaniec–Kowalski, *Analytic Number Theory* (AMS Colloq. Publ. 53, 2004), Theorem 7.13.**
   161	  For complex a_n with M < n ≤ M + N:
   162	    Σ_{q≤Q} (q/φ(q)) Σ*_{χ (mod q)} |Σ_n a_n χ(n)|² ≤ (Q² + N − 1) Σ|a_n|²,
   163	  where Σ* is over primitive characters. Quoted verbatim as Drappeau's Lemme 6, and as Pascadi's
   164	  Lemma 3.3 in arXiv v3, which is **Lemma 3.4 in the published version** (codex lem85_1, finding 12).
   165	  We apply it with Q = ⌊2R⌋, an integer.
   166	- **(P) Truncated Perron kernel — proved here, not cited.** For c > 0, T > 0 and w > 0 with w ≠ 1:
   167	    |(2πi)^{−1}∫_{c−iT}^{c+iT} w^s ds/s − 1_{w>1}| ≤ w^c/(πT|log w|).
   168	  *Proof.*
   169	  - The integrand w^s/s is meromorphic, with a single simple pole at s = 0 of residue 1.
   170	  - If w > 1, close the segment [c − iT, c + iT] by the rectangle with vertical side Re s = −σ₀,
   171	    σ₀ → ∞. The left side contributes ≤ w^{−σ₀}·2T/σ₀ → 0. Each horizontal side contributes
   172	    ≤ (2π)^{−1}∫_{−∞}^{c} w^σ dσ/T = w^c/(2πT log w). The residue theorem gives the claim.
   173	  - If w < 1, close to the right instead. There is no pole, and the same bound holds with |log w|. ∎
   174	  - (This is the standard lemma of Davenport, *Multiplicative Number Theory*, ch. 17, and of
   175	    Montgomery–Vaughan, *Multiplicative Number Theory I*, Cor. 5.3. We prove it so that nothing
   176	    unnumbered is cited.)
   177	- **(RS) Rosser–Schoenfeld, Illinois J. Math. 6 (1962), 64–94.** Numbering as commonly cited, not
   178	  re-checked; any Chebyshev-strength substitute changes only constants.
   179	  - Corollary 1: π(z) < 1.25506 z/log z for z > 1.
   180	  - Corollary 3: π(2z) − π(z) > 3z/(5 log z) for z ≥ 20.5.
   181	  - Theorem 15: n/φ(n) < e^γ log log n + 2.50637/log log n for n ≥ 3, except n = 223092870, which
   182	    needs 2.51. We use the 2.51 form. These statements were confirmed from secondary sources; the
   183	    "Corollary 3" label was not.
   184	
   185	Consequences used, valid in regime (R) for N ≥ N₀. Write β(z, y) := α(z, y), the saddle point.
   186	- **(α1)** 3/4 ≤ β(z, y) ≤ 1 for 2y ≤ z ≤ x.
   187	- **(α2)** 1 − β(z, y) ≤ (log(u log y) + C_α log log y)/log y for 2y ≤ z ≤ x, with C_α absolute.
   188	- Proof of (α2): by (HT) Theorem 2(i), α ≥ ᾱ(1 − C loglog y/log y) with ᾱ := log(1 + y/log z)/log y.
   189	  Also log(1 + y/log z) ≥ log y − log log z, and log z ≤ log x = u log y.
   190	- (α1) follows from (α2) in regime (R). The upper bound α ≤ 1 holds because y > log z, so that
   191	  Σ_{p≤y} log p/(p − 1) > log z (Mertens) and the defining equation forces α < 1. Only α ≥ 3/4 is
   192	  actually used.
   193	
