   195	
   196	## 3. Elementary lemmas (all proved here)
   197	
   198	**E1 (orthogonality majorant).** For (a, q) = 1, E(z, y; q, a) = φ(q)^{−1} Σ_{χ≠χ₀ (mod q)} χ̄(a)Ψ(z, y; χ).
   199	Hence max_{(a,q)=1} |E(z, y; q, a)| ≤ Δ(q) := φ(q)^{−1} Σ_{χ≠χ₀} |Ψ(z, y; χ)|.
   200	*Proof.* Orthogonality, together with the fact that Ψ(z, y; χ₀) = Ψ_q(z, y). ∎
   201	
   202	**E2 (lift averaging).** Let ℓ be a prime with ℓ > y, let s ≥ 1 with ℓ ∤ s, and put t = ℓs. For every
   203	r with (r, t) = 1:
   204	  E(z, y; t, r) = E(z, y; s, r)/(ℓ − 1) + Rem(t, r),
   205	  where |Rem(t, r)| ≤ Δ_ℓ(t) := φ(t)^{−1} Σ_{χ (mod t), ℓ | cond χ} |Ψ(z, y; χ)|.
   206	
   207	*Proof.* (ℤ/t)^* = (ℤ/ℓ)^* × (ℤ/s)^*, and every χ (mod t) factors as χ_ℓχ_s. Also ℓ | cond χ if and
   208	only if χ_ℓ ≠ χ₀^{(ℓ)}.
   209	- Every y-smooth n is coprime to ℓ. So for χ_ℓ = χ₀^{(ℓ)} we get Ψ(z, y; χ₀^{(ℓ)}χ_s) = Ψ(z, y; χ_s).
   210	  Also Ψ_t(z, y) = Ψ_s(z, y), and φ(t) = (ℓ − 1)φ(s).
   211	- In E1 for modulus t, the terms with χ_ℓ = χ₀^{(ℓ)} and χ_s ≠ χ₀^{(s)} sum to
   212	  ((ℓ−1)φ(s))^{−1} Σ_{χ_s≠χ₀} χ̄_s(r)Ψ(z, y; χ_s) = E(z, y; s, r)/(ℓ − 1).
   213	- The remaining terms have ℓ | cond χ.
   214	
   215	Equivalently, without characters: averaging E(z, y; t, ·) over the ℓ − 1 lifts of r (mod s) to
   216	(ℤ/t)^* gives exactly E(z, y; s, r)/(ℓ − 1). This is checked exactly in `tests/test_lem85.py`. ∎
   217	
   218	**E3 (coprimality by Möbius).** Let χ (mod q) be induced by a primitive χ* (mod r), and put
   219	q_y := ∏_{p|q, p≤y} p. Then
   220	  Ψ(z, y; χ) = Σ_{d | q_y, (d,r)=1} μ(d)χ*(d)Ψ(z/d, y; χ*).
   221	
   222	*Proof.* For y-smooth n, (n, q) = 1 if and only if (n, q_y) = 1. Expand 1_{(n,q_y)=1} = Σ_{d|(n,q_y)} μ(d).
   223	Then n = dn′ with n′ ∈ S(z/d, y), because d is y-smooth. Terms with (d, r) > 1 vanish. Checked
   224	exactly for real characters in the tests. ∎
   225	
   226	**E4 (factorisation).** Let M₁ ≥ 1. Every n ∈ S(z, y) with n > M₁ has exactly one factorisation n = mk
   227	with:
   228	- M₁ < m ≤ P⁺(m)M₁ (that is, m/P⁺(m) ≤ M₁ < m);
   229	- P⁺(m) ≤ P⁻(k);
   230	- k ∈ S(z, y).
   231	
   232	Moreover k ≤ z/M₁.
   233	
   234	*Proof.* Write n = p₁p₂⋯p_J with p₁ ≤ ⋯ ≤ p_J, and let m = p₁⋯p_j with j minimal such that the product
   235	exceeds M₁. Conversely, if (m, k) satisfies the three conditions, the primes of m come first in the
   236	sorted list of n's primes (ties at P⁺(m) = P⁻(k) are harmless). Then m/P⁺(m) ≤ M₁ < m forces j to be
   237	the number of prime factors of m. Checked exhaustively on toy ranges in the tests. ∎
   238	
   239	**E5.** Σ_{j≤X} 1/φ(j) ≤ 2(1 + log X) for X ≥ 1.
   240	*Proof.* j/φ(j) = Σ_{e|j} μ²(e)/φ(e). So the sum is at most (1 + log X)·Σ_e μ²(e)/(eφ(e)), and
   241	Σ_e μ²(e)/(eφ(e)) = ζ(2)ζ(3)/ζ(6) < 1.95. ∎
   242	
   243	**E6 (constants).** Over squarefree d:
   244	- Σ_d 1/(dφ(d)) < 1.95;
   245	- Σ_d 1/(φ(d)d^{1/2}) ≤ ∏_p (1 + 2p^{−3/2}) ≤ exp(2Σ_p p^{−3/2}) < e^{1.7} < 6, since Σ_p p^{−3/2} < 0.85;

