   679	\begin{lemma}[regularity; cf.\ {\cite{pollard1993}}]\label{lem:reg}
   680	Let $\lambda_1$ be the length of a shortest nonzero vector of $\Lambda_\ell(g)$. If $\lambda_1<\sqrt\ell/(2S)$, then
   681	$\Lambda_\ell(g)\cap\cP_\ell=\emptyset$. Otherwise $\#(\Lambda_\ell(g)\cap[-V_\ell,V_\ell]^2)\le200S^2$.
   682	\end{lemma}
   683	
   684	\begin{proof}
   685	Write $V=V_\ell$. Let $w_0$ be a shortest vector, with content $e\le|w_0|<\ell$. Then $e$ is invertible modulo $\ell$, so
   686	$w_0/e\in\Lambda_\ell(g)$, and minimality gives $e=1$. Thus $w_0$ is primitive in $\Lambda_\ell(g)$, and the lattice is
   687	the union of the lines $jw'+\Z w_0$ ($j\in\Z$), parallel to $w_0$ at spacing $\ell/|w_0|>2S\sqrt\ell\ge2V$. Every point
   688	of $[-V,V]^2$ has norm at most $\sqrt2V<2V$, so it lies on $\Z w_0$. The only primitive points there are
   689	$\pm w_0$, and $|w_0|<\sqrt\ell/(2S)<V/2<b$ for every $(a,b)\in\cP_\ell$. In the regular case take a reduced basis
   690	$\lambda_1\le\lambda_2$ with $\lambda_1\lambda_2\le(2/\sqrt3)\ell$. Translates of its fundamental parallelogram at the lattice points
   691	of $[-V,V]^2$ are disjoint and lie in a square of side $2V+4\lambda_2$. Since $V^2\le\ell S^2$ and
   692	$\lambda_2^2\le(4/3)\ell^2/\lambda_1^2\le(16/3)S^2\ell$, the count is at most $(2V+4\lambda_2)^2/\ell\le(8V^2+32\lambda_2^2)/\ell\le200S^2$.
   693	\end{proof}
