  1785	\begin{fact}[Stark {\cite[Lemma~8]{stark1974}}; {\cite[Fact~6.22]{lv2018}}]\label{fact:Stark}
  1820	\emph{Sampler.} Let $x$ be the least integer with $\log(x/2)\ge\max(20d(\log\bar\Delta)^2,(\log N)^2)$, so $\log x=O_d((\log N)^2)$. Draw $r$
  1821	uniform in $(x/2,x]$. If $r$ is prime (AKS), find the set $Z_r$ of roots of $f\bmod r$ (Las Vegas, e.g.\ Cantor--Zassenhaus
  1822	\cite{cantorzassenhaus1981}). With probability $|Z_r|/d$ output $(r,s)$ with $s$ uniform in $Z_r$; otherwise redraw. Each pair $(r,s)$ is output with the same
  1823	probability, so the output is uniform over the degree-one primes $\fp=(r,\alpha-s)$ with $\Norm\fp\in(x/2,x]$. Since $r>\bar\Delta\ge|D|$, $r>B$, and
  1824	$r$ exceeds every $|F_A(P)|$, no atom $a-b\alpha$ lies in $\fp$.
  1825	
  1826	The expected number of draws is $O(d\log x)$. To see this, apply Fact~\ref{fact:LO} to $K/K$ (trivial group, $n_L=d$). If $\zeta_K$ had a real zero in
  1827	$[1-(4\cdot d!\log|\Delta_K|)^{-1},1)$, Fact~\ref{fact:Stark} would produce a quadratic subfield of $K$, which does not exist. A zero in the wider
  1828	interval of Fact~\ref{fact:LO} contributes at most $x^{1-1/(4d!\log\bar\Delta)}=o(x/\log x)$ because $\log x\ge(\log N)^2$. So there are at least
  1829	$x/(3\log x)$ prime ideals of $K$ with norm in $(x/2,x]$; those of degree $\ge2$ number at most $d\sqrt x$.
