codex/lv5unif_1.out:1:**The apparent \(\tau=1\) barrier is removable.** After matching the smoothness densities and repairing the geometric bounds, the listed moment ingredients admit \(\tau=0\) at the \(2/5\) boundary. The shared-rational-side construction also has a valid amplification argument, but conditional independence alone does not establish it.
codex/lv5unif_1.out:48:Thus an exponential factor **in a norm bound** need not be an exponential precision loss. An exponential factor **multiplying an upper moment after normalization** is different.
codex/lv5unif_1.out:74:- The small representative construction for \(\phi_P\) has coordinate bound \(O(V)\), independent of \(d\). Its exponentially large cardinality is not a probability loss or an enumeration cost.
codex/lv5unif_1.out:188:Every degree-\((d-3)\) form with coefficients in \(\{-1,0,1\}\), multiplied by \(Q\), lies in \(\ker\Phi\cap C_H\), because
codex/lv5unif_1.out:215:is banded: each coordinate receives contributions from at most three basis vectors. Its fundamental parallelepiped therefore has \(\ell^\infty\)-radius at most \(4V^2\), **independent of \(d\)**.
codex/lv5unif_1.out:261:They tend to zero for the explicit parameters below. Narrowing \(J\) multiplies the conductor-\(\ell\) terms by \(O(d)\), which changes nothing.
codex/lv5unif_1.out:275:- **Prime multiplier:** Lemma W, discussed below.
codex/lv5unif_1.out:292:They are multiplicative moment discrepancies. The smoothness-density adjustment for the corresponding \(2^{O(d)}\) argument shift is only \(1+o(1)\), so it cannot cancel them.
codex/lv5unif_1.out:402:**Conditional independence is exact, but insufficient on its own.** Given \(g\), the field vectors generated by independent \((w_j,c_j)\) are independent. Different targets \(\ell\) within one field need not be independent.
codex/lv5unif_1.out:404:A counterexample to independence-only amplification is immediate: let every field succeed exactly on the same deterministic target subset of density \(\varepsilon\). The field vectors are independent—even deterministic—but any number of fields still covers density only \(\varepsilon\).
codex/lv5unif_1.out:445:Taking \(t=\lceil96KP^7\rceil\), conditional independence gives coverage probability at least \(1-e^{-1}\) on this subset. Thus the averaged covered density is \(\gg P^{-3}\). ∎
codex/lv5unif_1.out:457:There is a post-processing repair too. With \(t\) fields, there can be \(t\) residual exceptional bits, so three dependency tests no longer suffice. Choose \(t+1\) independent kernel vectors. Exact square tests in each field label these vectors by \(t\) binary residual coordinates; solve the resulting binary system and obtain a nonzero combination square in every field. Verification remains exact. All additional work and columns contribute \(\exp(o(\Lambda_\alpha))\).
codex/lv5unif_1.out:459:**Conclusion:** the shared-side trick can remove algebraic precision losses. It does not change the one-level norm geometry, and therefore does not get below \(2/5\).
codex/lv5unif_1.out:597:The \(2/5\) geometric floor remains: the shared-side trick changes precision, not the inequalities \(d\gtrsim D^{1-2\alpha}\), \(k\gtrsim d^2\). Below it, nested descendability remains **open**.
